<title>Exploring an anteater dynamical system</title>
<variantControl numVariants="1" />

<description>
Exploring a model of anteater population growth using interactive applets.
</description>

<setup>
  <number name="stage">1</number>
  <number name="r">0.0000006</number>
  <number name="alpha">2000</number>
  <number name="beta">500</number>
  <number name="tmax">20</number>
  <number name="xmax">3200</number>
  <number fixed="true" name="zeroFixed">0</number>
  
  <clampNumber name="x0" lowerValue="0" upperValue="$xmax">100</clampNumber>

  <odeSystem name="ode" initialconditions="$x0" tolerance="0.01" displaydigits="6">
    <rightHandSide>$r*x*(x-$alpha)($beta-x)</rightHandSide>
  </odeSystem>

  <function extend="$ode.numericalSolution" name="f" />


  <animateFromSequence name="timeAnimation" target="$t.value" from="0" to="$tmax" step="0.1" animationInterval="100" animationMode="increase once" animationOn="false" />

</setup>


<p>Let's use an anteater model to explore some basic ideas about the 
<ref to="https://mathinsight.org/dynamical_system_idea">idea of a dynamical system</ref>, which is a system where things change over time according to some fixed rule. We will use 
a simulation to visualize concepts such as the 
<term><ref to="doenet:82MrUSX6Y2kQrMerT3xvqn">state space</ref></term>
 of a dynamical system and the rule for the evolution of the 
<term><ref to="doenet:82S5kKvMFZgTae6rHgvyF4">state variables</ref></term>.</p>



<section><title>The state variable</title>


<image source="doenet:xouB7o7pjQxxgyqrTHNu49" asfilename="giant_anteater.jpg" width="300" aspectRatio="640/396" mimeType="image/jpeg" imageName="A giant anteater" authorName="Duane Nykamp" licenseCodes="CC-BY"><shortDescription>A giant anteater</shortDescription></image>

<p>The first step in defining a dynamical system is to determine the 
<term><ref to="doenet:82S5kKvMFZgTae6rHgvyF4">state variables</ref></term>.  To keep it simple, we'll use a single state variable <m>a</m> to represent the total number of anteaters.  Since a negative population size doesn't make sense, the 
<term><ref to="doenet:82MrUSX6Y2kQrMerT3xvqn">state space</ref></term>
 will be the set of non-negative numbers, which we can represent by a number line.</p>



<p>The number <m>a</m> of anteaters fluctuates as anteaters are born and die.  We'll capture how the value of <m>a</m> changes as a function of <m>t</m>, where <m>t</m> denotes time in years.  The notation <m>a(t)</m> represents the population size in year <m>t</m>.</p>

<p>If year <m>t=0</m> is the year we started modeling the anteater population, then  <m>a(10.1532)=132</m> indicates that the anteater population contains 132 anteaters after 10.1532 years after we started the modeling.</p>


<p>We skipped over some implicit assumptions we made to keep the state space simple.  You can check out the following optional sections if you are curious.</p>

<aside name="singleVariableAssumptions">
  <title>Ignoring details to get a single variable</title>

  <p>Since we have just one state variable <m>a</m>, we assume that the only thing that matters is the total number of anteaters.  We're ignoring any possibility that the growth of the population could depend on other details, such as the ages or sexes of the anteaters, or the distances between the anteaters.  We're also assuming we don't have to worry about the availability of food or the influence of any predators.</p>

  <p>Oh well.  Let's not try to justify these choices, other than to say we want to keep the model simple.</p>
</aside>

<aside name="fractional_anteaters"><title>Fractional anteaters?</title>

<image source="doenet:mpGGmnsVZydsV14sUBhXor" asfilename="3_29_anteaters.png" width="300" aspectRatio="512/231" mimeType="image/png" imageName="3.29 anteaters?" authorName="Duane Nykamp" licenseCodes="CC-BY"><shortDescription>3.29 anteaters?</shortDescription></image>
  
 <p>As far as we know, the number of anteaters <m>a</m> should be an integer.  But, let's not let reality get in our way.  Bring on the fractional anteaters!  It will make our lives much easier, as the math will be simpler.  Have no fear if the model says we have <m>a=3.29</m> anteaters.  In that case, you could just imagine that we must have 3 anteaters.  Or work on imagining what 0.29 of an anteater would look like.</p>
 
</aside>


<aside><title>State variable notation: with or without <m>t</m>?</title>

<p>Sometimes we refer to the state variable using just the letter <m>a</m>, but at other times, we might refer to it as <m>a(t)</m> to emphasize that it depends on time or to give a specific time like <m>a(11)</m>.  We hope that's not too confusing!  For this model <m>a</m> and <m>a(t)</m> both mean the anteater population size.</p>

</aside>

<section><title>Your turn</title>

<p>Using this state variable notation, write an equation stating that we start out with 512 anteaters.</p>

<setup>
  <math name="a_of_something" functionSymbols="a">a()</math>
  <math name="a_of_0" functionSymbols="a">a(0)</math>
</setup>

<p><answer name="start512">
  <mathInput name="start512Input" functionSymbols="a" />
  <award><when>$start512Input = <math>$a_of_0 = 512</math></when></award>
  <award credit="0" name="have_a_of_0">
    <when>
      <matchesPattern name="match_a_of_0" pattern="$a_of_0 = ()">
        $start512Input
      </matchesPattern>
    </when>
  </award>
  <award credit="0" name="have_a_of_something">
    <when>
      <matchesPattern name="match_a_of_something" pattern="$a_of_something = ()">
        $start512Input
      </matchesPattern>
    </when>
  </award>

  <award credit="0" name="just_have_equation"><when>
    <matchesPattern pattern="()=()" name="matchEquation">$start512Input</matchesPattern>
    </when></award>
  <award credit="0" name="no_equation"><when>
    not $matchEquation
    </when></award>
  </answer></p>

<hint hide="$start512.numSubmissions &lt; 3 or $start512.numSubmissions > 4 or $have_a_of_0">
  <p>Your answer should be an equation for the initial population size <m>a(0)</m>, i.e., it should look like <m>a(0) = </m> something.</p>
</hint>
<hint hide="$start512.numSubmissions &lt; 5 or $start512.creditAchieved = 1">
  <p>The equation stating that we start out with 512 anteaters is
  <me>a(0)=512</me></p>
</hint>


<feedback condition="$no_equation">
  We are expecting an equation with an equal sign.
</feedback>
<feedback condition="$just_have_equation">
  You should have an equation in the same form as the example: <m>a(10.1532)=132</m>.
</feedback>
  
<feedback condition="$have_a_of_something">
  Good start. You have an equation involving <m>a($match_a_of_something.patternMatch1)</m>, which is the population size at time <m>t=$match_a_of_something.patternMatch1</m>.
      But, we are specifying initial population size, i.e., population size at time <m>t=0</m>.
</feedback>
<feedback condition="$have_a_of_0">
  <math extend="$match_a_of_0.patternMatch1" name="a_0_value" hide="true" />
  <conditionalContent>
    <case condition="not isnumber($a_0_value)">
      Getting close!  On one side of the equation you have the expression for the initial anteater population <m>a(0)</m>.  Now, on the other side, you need a number, which is the actual number of anteaters at time <m>t=0</m>.
    </case>
    <else>
      The equation $start512 says that we start out with <pluralize>$a_0_value anteater</pluralize>.  How many anteaters are we supposed to start with?
    </else>
  </conditionalContent>

</feedback>


<p hide="$start512.creditAchieved &lt; 1">
  Great! Let's take a look at the anteater model.
</p>

<updateValue disabled="$start512.creditAchieved &lt; 1" target="$stage" newValue="2" hide="$stage >=2">
  <label>continue</label>
</updateValue>
</section>

</section>

<section hide="$stage &lt; 2"><title>Anteater dynamics viewed on the state line</title>

<p>The next step in creating a dynamical system model is to define the rule for how the state variable <m>a</m> changes.  But, we're going to keep it a secret because the rule involves <ref to="doenet:7JYpAstsJwNzaFaKUGWwWe">derivatives</ref> and we don't expect you to know derivatives.  Instead, we'll explore a simulation to see the behavior of the model.  (Or, you can cheat and look at <ref to="doenet:7BXVp6EG6LArNJ3qrYoj5R">the full simulation</ref> to see the equation.)</p>


<subsection><title>Representing population size on the state line</title>

<p>The below <text extend="$stateLine1.styleDescription" /> line is supposed to represent the state space of our anteater model.  <span hide="$userStateVar = a">But wait, there's something wrong. The state line is labeled by the variable $userStateVarInput.immediateValue rather than the state variable for the anteater population.  We can't go on until you fix that.</span></p>

<p> State variable for anteater population size:
<answer name="userStateVar"><mathInput name="userStateVarInput" prefill="x" />
<award><math>a</math></award>
</answer> </p>


<hint hide="$userStateVar.numSubmissions &lt; 3 or $userStateVar.creditAchieved = 1">
  <p>We are using the state variable <m>a</m> to represent the anteater population size.</p>
</hint>


<p hide="not ($userStateVar = a)">Good, now the <text extend="$stateLine1.styleDescription" /> line represents the state space of our anteater model.  The state variable <m>a</m> can be any non-negative real number.</p>
<text hide="true" name="textUserStateVarInput">$userStateVarInput.immediateValue</text>

  
    
<graph displayYAxis="false" size="medium" aspectRatio="300/60" xmin="-0.05$xmax" xmax="$xmax" showNavigation="false"> <xLabel>$textUserStateVarInput</xLabel>


  <lineSegment endpoints="(0,0) ($xmax, 0)" fixed="true" name="stateLine1" />
  <point x="$x0" y="$zeroFixed" name="x0onLine1" hide="not $showA0onLine1" labelPosition="lowerright" applyStyleToLabel="true">
    
      <constrainToGrid />
    
    <label><m>a(0)=$x0</m></label>
  </point>
  <point styleNumber="3" x="$$f($t)" y="$zeroFixed" draggable="false" layer="1" hide="not $showAOfTOnLine1" name="aOfTOnLine1" applyStyleToLabel="true">
    <label><m>$aOfTDisplay</m></label>
  </point>
</graph>

<div hide="$userStateVar != a">

<p>The first thing we'll do with the state line is represent the initial population size <m>a(0)</m>, which we call the <term>initial condition</term>.  Check this box to reveal a <text extend="$x0onLine1.styleDescriptionWithNoun" /> to denote the initial condition <booleanInput name="showA0onLine1" />.</p>

<p>You can change the initial condition by dragging the <text extend="$x0onLine1.styleDescriptionWithNoun" /> or by entering a value below.</p>

<p><m>a(0) = </m> <mathInput bindValueTo="$x0" /></p>

<p>Next, we'll represent the population at time <m>t</m> by a <text extend="$aOfTOnLine1.styleDescriptionWithNoun" />, which you can reveal by checking this box: <booleanInput name="showAOfTOnLine1" />.</p>

<p>You can't move the <text extend="$aOfTOnLine1.styleDescriptionWithNoun" /> directly as it is determined by the dynamics of the anteater model.  To see how the population size changes with time, change the time <m>t</m> via the below slider, or you can animate time with this button. 
  <updateValue name="on1" hide="$timeAnimation.animationOn" type="boolean" target="$timeAnimation.animationOn" newValue="true">
    <label>Animate</label>
  </updateValue>
<updateValue name="off1" hide="not $timeAnimation.animationOn" type="boolean" target="$timeAnimation.animationOn" newValue="false">
  <label>Stop</label>
</updateValue></p>

<slider name="t" width="200px" from="0" to="$tmax" step="0.1" labelIsName="true" />

<p>The population size <m>a(t)</m> at time <m>t</m> is displayed next to the <text extend="$aOfTOnLine1.styleDescriptionWithNoun" /> on the state line and repeated here.</p>

<p><math displaydecimals="1" functionSymbols="a" name="aOfTDisplay">a($t) = $$f($t)</math></p>

</div>
</subsection>

<subsection hide="$userStateVar != a"><title>Exploring the population behavior</title>

<odeSystem name="ode512" initialconditions="512" tolerance="0.01" displaydigits="6" hide="true">
  <rightHandSide>$r*x*(x-$alpha)($beta-x)</rightHandSide>
</odeSystem>
<function extend="$ode512.numericalSolution" name="f512" hide="true" />


<p>Let's see what happens when we have an initial population size of <m>a(0)=512</m> anteaters.  If we start with 512 anteaters, how many anteaters does the model predict there will be after one year? 
<answer name="a1from512">
  <award allowedErrorInNumbers="0.1" allowedErrorIsAbsolute="true">$$f512(1)</award>
  <award credit="0.8" allowedErrorInNumbers="1" allowedErrorIsAbsolute="true" feedbackText="Close, but you need to be within 0.1 from the correct answer.">$$f512(1)</award>
</answer>
</p>

$a1from512.feedbacks

<hint hide="$a1from512.creditAchieved = 1">
  To determine how many anteaters there will be after one year, set the initial condition to <m>a(0)=512</m> and change time via the slider to <m>t=1</m>.  Then read off the population size <m>a(1)</m> from the <text extend="$aOfTOnLine1.styleDescriptionWithNoun" /> on the state line or from below the slider for <m>t</m>.
</hint>

<p>How many anteaters will there be after 5 years?
<answer name="a5from512">
  <award allowedErrorInNumbers="0.1" allowedErrorIsAbsolute="true">$$f512(5)</award>
  <award credit="0.8" name="roundedA5from512"><round>$$f512(5)</round></award>
  <award credit="0.8" allowedErrorInNumbers="1" allowedErrorIsAbsolute="true" feedbackText="Close, but you need to be accurate to one decimal.">$$f512(5)</award>
</answer>
</p>
$a5from512.feedbacks
<feedback condition="$roundedA5from512">
  As mentioned in the <ref to="$fractional_anteaters">optional section on fractional anteaters</ref>, we allow our model to have fractional anteaters, as it makes the mathematics simpler.  For full credit, include one decimal in the population size.
</feedback>

<p>The simulation won't let you take time all the way to infinity, but you can get a pretty good idea about the what happens to the population size in the long run, given that we started with 512 anteaters.  After a long time, the population
<answer name="a512longTimeMC">
  <choiceInput shuffleOrder="true" inline="true">
    <choice credit="1">tends to a positive number.</choice>
    <choice>goes extinct.</choice>
    <choice>blows up.</choice>
    <choice>oscillates.</choice>
    <choice>goes out to lunch.</choice>
  </choiceInput>
</answer>
<span hide="$a512longTimeMC.creditAchieved &lt; 1">In fact, the population size tends to the number <answer allowedErrorInNumbers="0.1" allowedErrorIsAbsolute="true" name="a512longTimeVal"><award>$$f512(20)</award></answer>.
</span>
</p>

<div hide="$a512longTimeVal.creditAchieved &lt; 1">
<p>Is <m>a(0) = 512</m> the only initial condition that leads to 2000 anteaters in the long run?  <answer><choiceInput inline="true"><choice>yes</choice><choice credit="1">no</choice></choiceInput></answer>  Explore the behavior of the model to find which initial conditions lead to a long term population size of 2000 anteaters.  Write your answer as an inequality involving <m>a(0)</m>, such as <m>a(0)&lt; 7</m> or <m>8 &lt; a(0) &lt; 11</m> or <m>a(0) > 13</m>.</p>

<p>The anteater population tends to 2000 if the initial condition satisfies 
  <answer name="a0sTo2000">
    <mathInput functionSymbols="a" name="a0sTo2000Input" />
    <award><when>$a0sTo2000Input  = <math functionSymbols="a">a(0) > 500</math></when></award>
    <award credit=".9" feedbackText="Close, but does the population size approach 2000 if we start with exactly 500 anteaters?"><when>$a0sTo2000Input  = <math functionSymbols="a">a(0) >= 500</math></when></award>
    <award credit=".9" name="a0sTo2000Round"><when>$a0sTo2000Input  = <math functionSymbols="a">a(0) >= 501</math></when></award>
    <award credit=".8" feedbackText="Getting there.  But what happens if we start with, for example, 501 or 500.9 anteaters?"><when>$a0sTo2000Input  = <math functionSymbols="a">a(0) > 501</math></when></award>
  </answer>.
</p>

$a0sTo2000.feedbacks
<feedback condition="$a0sTo2000Round">
That would be true if the model allowed only integer numbers of anteaters.  But, as mentioned in the <ref to="$fractional_anteaters">optional fractional anteaters section</ref>, we allow our model to have fractional anteaters.  What happens if we start with, for example, 500.9 anteaters?
</feedback>

<hint hide="$a0sTo2000.numSubmissions &lt; 3 or $a0sTo2000.numSubmissions > 4 or $a0sTo2000.creditAchieved = 1">
  <p>It turns out that the number of anteaters approaches 2000 as long as we start out with a sufficient number of anteaters.  What is the threshold the population size much exceed to reach 2000?</p>
</hint>

<hint hide="$a0sTo2000.numSubmissions &lt; 5 or $a0sTo2000.creditAchieved = 1">
  <p>You should find that the population size <m>a(t)</m> tends to 2000 as long as <m>a(0) > 500</m>.</p>
</hint>


<p hide="$a0sTo2000.creditAchieved &lt; 1">
  Fantastic!  Let's add more ways of visualizing the simulation results.
</p>


</div>


</subsection>

<updateValue disabled="$a0sTo2000.creditAchieved &lt; 1" target="$stage" newValue="3" hide="$stage != 2">
  <label>continue</label>
</updateValue>


</section>

<section hide="$stage &lt; 3"><title>Anteater population viewed versus time</title>

<p>We can plot the population size <m>a(t)</m> as a function of time by rotating the <text extend="$stateLine2.styleDescription" /> line representing the state space to be vertical and using the horizontal axis for time, as shown below.</p>


<p hide="$userStateVar2 = a and $userTimeVar2 = t">Sorry, we seem to have made a mistake in labeling the graph again.  The vertical axis should be labeled by the state variable and the horizontal axis should be labeled by the time variable.  We're relying on you to set us straight.</p>

<p>Variable for horizontal axis:
<answer name="userTimeVar2"><mathInput name="userTimeVar2Input" prefill="x" />
<award><math>t</math></award>
</answer>. Variable for vertical axis:
<answer name="userStateVar2"><mathInput name="userStateVar2Input" prefill="y" />
<award><math>a</math></award>
</answer>. </p>

<hint hide="$userTimeVar2.numSubmissions &lt; 3 or $userTimeVar2.creditAchieved = 1">
  <p>Use the time variable <m>t</m> for the horizontal axis.</p>
</hint>
<hint hide="$userStateVar2.numSubmissions &lt; 3 or $userStateVar2.creditAchieved = 1">
  <p>Use the state variable <m>a</m> for the vertical axis.</p>
</hint>


<p hide="($userStateVar2 != a) or ($userTimeVar2 != t)">Great, we're set up to plot the state variable <m>a</m> versus time <m>t</m>.</p>


<graph xmax="$tmax" xmin="-0.1$tmax" ymax="$xmax" ymin="-0.1$xmax" showNavigation="false">
  <xLabel>$userTimeVar2Input.immediateValue</xLabel>
  <yLabel>$userStateVar2Input.immediateValue</yLabel>
  <lineSegment endpoints="(0,0) (0, $xmax)" fixed="true" name="stateLine2" />

  <function extend="$f" stylenumber="2" name="aOfT1" hide="not $showAOfTFunction1" />
  <point x="$zeroFixed" y="$x0" name="x0onLine2">
    
      <constrainToGrid />
    
  </point>
  <point styleNumber="3" x="$t" y="$$f($t)" layer="1" name="aOfTOnCurve1" hide="not $showAOfTOnCurve1">
    <label><m>$aOfTDisplay</m></label>
  </point>

</graph>

<div hide="($userStateVar2 != a) or ($userTimeVar2 != t)">

<p>Now that we have another dimension of graph for time, we can explicitly show the entire function <m>a(t)</m> for <m>0 \lt t \lt $tmax</m>.  Check the box to reveal the <text extend="$aOfT1.styleDescription" /> curve representing <m>a(t)</m>  <booleanInput name="showAOfTFunction1" disabled="$showAOfTFunction1" />.  By dragging the <text extend="$x0onLine2.styleDescription" /> point, you can change the initial condition <m>a(0)</m> and immediately observe the effect on the evolution of the population size <m>a(t)</m>.  For finer grain control of the initial condition, you can type a value, below.</p>

<p><m>a(0) = </m> <mathInput bindValueTo="$x0" /></p>


<p hide="$showAOfTFunction1">Check the box from the previous paragraph to continue...</p>

<p hide="not $showAOfTFunction1">You can also show the <text extend="$aOfTOnCurve1.styleDescriptionWithNoun" /> representing the population size at a given time <m>t</m> by checking the box  <booleanInput name="showAOfTOnCurve1" />.  In this case, you can change time <m>t</m> by dragging the <text extend="$aOfTOnCurve1.styleDescriptionWithNoun" />.  Or you can use the controls for <m>t</m> from the previous section (the slider or animation button).  The population size <m>a(t)</m> at time <m>t</m> is displayed next to the <text extend="$aOfTOnCurve1.styleDescriptionWithNoun" /> and repeated below.</p>

<p hide="not $showAOfTFunction1">$aOfTDisplay</p>

<subsection hide="not $showAOfTFunction1"><title>Further exploration of anteater model</title>

<odeSystem name="ode423" initialconditions="423" tolerance="0.01" displaydigits="6" hide="true">
  <rightHandSide>$r*x*(x-$alpha)($beta-x)</rightHandSide>
</odeSystem>
<function extend="$ode423.numericalSolution" name="f423" hide="true" />

<p>For the initial condition <m>a(0)=423</m>, how many anteaters will there be after four years? 
<answer name="a4from423">
  <award allowedErrorInNumbers="0.1" allowedErrorIsAbsolute="true">$$f423(4)</award>
  <award credit="0.8" name="roundedA4from423"><round>$$f423(4)</round></award>
  <award credit="0.8" allowedErrorInNumbers="1" allowedErrorIsAbsolute="true" feedbackText="Close, but you need to be accurate to one decimal.">$$f423(4)</award>
</answer>
</p>

$a4from423.feedbacks
<feedback condition="$roundedA4from423">
  As mentioned in the <ref to="$fractional_anteaters">optional section on fractional anteaters</ref>, we allow our model to have fractional anteaters, as it makes the mathematics simpler.  For full credit, include one decimal in the population size.
</feedback>


<p>If we start with 423 anteaters, the population eventually
<answer name="a423longTimeMC">
  <choiceInput shuffleOrder="true" inline="true">
    <choice>tends to a positive number.</choice>
    <choice credit="1">goes extinct.</choice>
    <choice>blows up.</choice>
    <choice>oscillates.</choice>
    <choice>dances in the street.</choice>
  </choiceInput>
</answer>
</p>

<p>You should have discovered a very different fate for the anteaters when starting with 423 anteaters versus 512 anteaters.  There seems to be some tipping point, below which the anteaters cannot survive.  What is the critical population size that the anteaters must exceed in order to survive?  
<m>a = </m> <answer name="critNum">500</answer>
</p>


<hint hide="$critNum.numSubmissions &lt; 3 or $critNum.numSubmissions > 4 or $critNum.creditAchieved = 1">
  <p>The number of anteaters approaches 2000 when we start above this number.  The number of anteaters approaches zero when we start below this number.  What is the critical number that separates these two outcomes?</p>
</hint>

<hint hide="$critNum.numSubmissions &lt; 5 or $critNum.creditAchieved = 1">
  <p>You should find that the population size <m>a(t)</m> tends to 2000 when <m>a(0) \gt 500</m> and that the population size <m>a(t)</m> tends to 0 when <m>a(0) \lt 500</m>.  Therefore the critical population size that the anteaters must exceed to survive is 500.</p>
</hint>

<p>When <m>a(0)</m> is exactly at this critical population, what happens to the population?  The population
<answer>
  <choiceInput shuffleOrder="true" inline="true">
    <choice credit="1">stays at that critical size.</choice>
    <choice>goes extinct.</choice>
    <choice>blows up.</choice>
    <choice>oscillates.</choice>
    <choice>stands at attention.</choice>
  </choiceInput>
</answer></p>

<p>
The simple model we're using for the anteater dynamics can't answer the following question.  But maybe you have some ideas.  What biological factors could lead to such a critical population size where a population goes extinct if it gets too small?
</p>

<textInput expanded="true" />

<p>If you like, you can further explore the mathematical model used for the anteater population on its <ref to="doenet:7BXVp6EG6LArNJ3qrYoj5R">simulation page</ref>.</p>

</subsection>


</div>


</section>
